1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,013
The team did the careful thing. They
wrote a filter that removes outputs from

2
00:00:09,053 --> 00:00:13,862
notebooks on the way into the repository.
A notebook is a document that keeps code

3
00:00:13,902 --> 00:00:17,470
together with its results. They
committed the attribute line.

4
00:00:17,510 --> 00:00:20,840
They tested it. For six months
no output reached history.

5
00:00:21,180 --> 00:00:25,278
Then a new engineer cloned the repository,
ran a notebook, and committed.

6
00:00:25,318 --> 00:00:29,472
Her clone had the attribute. git
check-attr in her clone names the filter.

7
00:00:29,512 --> 00:00:33,724
And Git said nothing at all while it
stored 40 megabytes of rendered plots.

8
00:00:34,064 --> 00:00:38,379
The rule did apply to her. The rule
says "use the filter named nbstrip".

9
00:00:38,419 --> 00:00:43,400
Her machine had no filter of that name, and
Git treats that as "no filter", without

10
00:00:43,440 --> 00:00:45,397
a warning. She did nothing wrong.

11
00:00:47,237 --> 00:00:50,888
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:50,928 --> 00:00:55,911
In the last video .gitattributes named
properties that every Git understands: text,

13
00:00:55,951 --> 00:01:00,994
eol, export-ignore. Today it names programs.
There are three places where Git lets a

14
00:01:01,034 --> 00:01:06,016
program of yours take part: when it shows
a change, when it merges a file, and when

15
00:01:06,056 --> 00:01:09,526
content moves between the
working tree and the repository.

16
00:01:09,866 --> 00:01:14,357
For all three, the same rule holds, and
it's the rule that explains the hook.

17
00:01:14,397 --> 00:01:17,652
The name is in a versioned
file and reaches every clone.

18
00:01:17,692 --> 00:01:20,830
The definition is in
configuration and reaches nobody.

19
00:01:20,870 --> 00:01:25,714
One question to keep for later: would marking
the filter as required have saved the

20
00:01:25,754 --> 00:01:26,244
new hire?

21
00:01:26,584 --> 00:01:31,462
The third mechanism, the filter, is also
the foundation of two things later in this

22
00:01:31,502 --> 00:01:35,135
course: Git LFS, and notebook
stripping in the AI and ML part.

23
00:01:36,975 --> 00:01:40,853
make a binary or generated format
diffable with a textconv driver;

24
00:01:41,193 --> 00:01:46,753
choose merge=union, -merge or a custom merge
driver for a file and state the risk of

25
00:01:46,793 --> 00:01:47,086
each;

26
00:01:47,426 --> 00:01:51,780
write a clean and smudge filter pair and
show what is stored and what is checked

27
00:01:51,820 --> 00:01:51,999
out;

28
00:01:52,339 --> 00:01:56,065
explain what a clone receives of a
filter and what required changes;

29
00:01:56,405 --> 00:01:59,258
name where this mechanism is
used later in the course.

30
00:02:01,098 --> 00:02:06,611
Diff drivers. In one sentence: the diff
attribute tells Git how to show changes to a

31
00:02:06,651 --> 00:02:11,966
path. Not at all, with -diff. With a named
built-in pattern set for hunk headers,

32
00:02:12,006 --> 00:02:17,453
such as diff=python. Or through a driver
that you define in configuration, of which

33
00:02:17,493 --> 00:02:19,634
textconv is the most useful kind.

34
00:02:19,974 --> 00:02:25,177
A textconv program receives the name of a
temporary file and prints a text rendering

35
00:02:25,217 --> 00:02:29,984
of it. Git then diffs the renderings.
The conversion is one-way and meant for

36
00:02:30,024 --> 00:02:34,852
people. git diff, git show and git log
-p use it. git format-patch never does,

37
00:02:34,892 --> 00:02:38,722
because a patch made from converted
text could not be applied.

38
00:02:38,762 --> 00:02:42,592
A diff driver changes what is
displayed, never what is stored.

39
00:02:42,932 --> 00:02:47,527
Merge drivers. In one sentence: the merge
attribute chooses the file-level merge for

40
00:02:47,567 --> 00:02:50,727
a path when both sides changed
it. There are four choices.

41
00:02:50,767 --> 00:02:55,197
The normal three-way text merge, which
combines two versions against their common

42
00:02:55,237 --> 00:02:59,776
base. A refusal to merge by content, -merge.
The built-in union driver, which keeps

43
00:02:59,816 --> 00:03:02,535
the lines of both sides.
Or a command of your own.

44
00:03:02,875 --> 00:03:08,173
A custom driver is a configuration
section with a driver command line.

45
00:03:08,213 --> 00:03:14,349
Git calls it with temporary files for the
three versions: %O for the base, %A for

46
00:03:14,389 --> 00:03:20,678
the current branch, %B for the other branch.
The driver leaves the result in %A and

47
00:03:20,718 --> 00:03:25,711
exits with status 0 for a clean
merge, or 1 to 128 for a conflict.

48
00:03:25,751 --> 00:03:31,277
The attribute applies to merges,
cherry-picks, reverts and rebases alike.

49
00:03:31,617 --> 00:03:36,203
The risks. The manual warns that union
"tends to leave the added lines in the

50
00:03:36,243 --> 00:03:40,948
resulting file in random order", and it
keeps both versions of a line that both

51
00:03:40,988 --> 00:03:44,793
sides edited. So it can produce
a wrong file without a conflict.

52
00:03:44,833 --> 00:03:47,917
A keep-ours driver silently
discards the other side.

53
00:03:47,957 --> 00:03:52,602
And -merge gives you a conflict every
time, which for some files is the honest

54
00:03:52,642 --> 00:03:53,023
answer.

55
00:03:53,363 --> 00:03:57,261
Filters. In one sentence: a filter
driver is a pair of commands.

56
00:03:57,301 --> 00:04:01,754
clean rewrites a file's content on its
way from the working tree into the

57
00:04:01,794 --> 00:04:06,800
repository, at git add. smudge rewrites it
on its way from the repository into the

58
00:04:06,840 --> 00:04:11,969
working tree, at checkout. The blob, the
stored bytes of the file, holds the cleaned

59
00:04:12,009 --> 00:04:12,277
form.

60
00:04:12,617 --> 00:04:17,502
Each command reads the content on standard
input and writes the converted content to

61
00:04:17,542 --> 00:04:22,251
standard output. Either half may be missing.
A clean filter should be idempotent:

62
00:04:22,291 --> 00:04:27,117
cleaning cleaned content changes nothing.
On the way in, the filter runs before the

63
00:04:27,157 --> 00:04:29,989
line-ending conversion.
On the way out, after it.

64
00:04:30,329 --> 00:04:35,250
The manual distinguishes two purposes.
Convenience filters make content nicer, and

65
00:04:35,290 --> 00:04:40,029
the project stays usable without them.
Every filter is of this kind by default.

66
00:04:40,369 --> 00:04:44,340
In the manual's words, "a missing filter
driver definition in the config, or a

67
00:04:44,380 --> 00:04:48,351
filter driver that exits with a non-zero
status, is not an error but makes the

68
00:04:48,391 --> 00:04:51,694
filter a no-op passthru." That's
the new hire's clone, on screen.

69
00:04:52,034 --> 00:04:57,238
Required filters turn stored content
that is unusable by itself, a pointer or

70
00:04:57,278 --> 00:05:02,550
ciphertext, into the real thing. With
filter.<name>.required = true, a failing

71
00:05:02,590 --> 00:05:06,568
filter becomes an error. Git
LFS is the best-known example.

72
00:05:06,908 --> 00:05:09,968
Why doesn't Git let the
repository define the commands?

73
00:05:10,008 --> 00:05:14,308
Because that would let anyone whose
repository you clone run programs on your

74
00:05:14,348 --> 00:05:18,423
machine. The line between "versioned"
and "local" is a security boundary.

75
00:05:18,763 --> 00:05:23,605
When not to use these. A textconv program
runs with your permissions on every diff

76
00:05:23,645 --> 00:05:27,236
of a matching file, and a rendering
hides what it leaves out.

77
00:05:27,276 --> 00:05:32,058
A custom merge driver exists only in the
clones that defined it, and merges for a

78
00:05:32,098 --> 00:05:37,059
pull request run on GitHub's servers, where
your driver configuration doesn't exist.

79
00:05:37,099 --> 00:05:41,880
And a filter changes the meaning of "clean
working tree": it now means "cleans to

80
00:05:41,920 --> 00:05:43,011
what is committed".

81
00:05:44,851 --> 00:05:49,158
The textbook's analogy for a filter: a
customs desk between your files and the

82
00:05:49,198 --> 00:05:53,504
object database. Goods are repacked on
the way in and unpacked on the way out.

83
00:05:53,844 --> 00:05:58,069
Where it breaks: every traveller
brings a desk of their own, or none.

84
00:05:58,109 --> 00:06:02,767
There is no shared customs office. A
traveller without a desk walks straight

85
00:06:02,807 --> 00:06:04,683
through, and nobody stops them.

86
00:06:05,023 --> 00:06:11,733
For all three drivers, keep the
folder labels from the last video.

87
00:06:11,773 --> 00:06:19,200
The label "show through viewer X" is
in the cabinet for everyone to read.

88
00:06:19,240 --> 00:06:27,382
Viewer X is a program on one person's
desk. And the last video's warning now has

89
00:06:27,422 --> 00:06:32,803
consequences: a missing
viewer produces no complaint.

90
00:06:34,643 --> 00:06:39,850
Try it now, on paper. Two columns: travels
with a clone, or stays on this machine.

91
00:06:39,890 --> 00:06:44,905
Place three things: the attribute line,
the clean command, and required = true.

92
00:06:44,945 --> 00:06:48,104
Pause me for thirty seconds
and write your answer.

93
00:06:52,944 --> 00:06:58,016
The two lines at the bottom are the whole
lesson. The first line is in a tracked

94
00:06:58,056 --> 00:07:00,252
file. The second is in .git/config.

95
00:07:00,592 --> 00:07:05,412
The last row surprises people. required
protects against a filter that is defined

96
00:07:05,452 --> 00:07:09,491
and fails. It doesn't protect against
a clone that never defined it.

97
00:07:09,531 --> 00:07:14,351
So the answer to the question from the
start is no: required would not have saved

98
00:07:14,391 --> 00:07:15,131
the new hire.

99
00:07:19,971 --> 00:07:24,950
Three changed lines, of which one matters:
the edit is mixed with a new execution

100
00:07:24,990 --> 00:07:30,092
count and new output. Name a driver in
.gitattributes, and predict whether the diff

101
00:07:30,132 --> 00:07:32,323
changes. Say it out loud. I'll wait.

102
00:07:37,163 --> 00:07:41,055
git check-attr reports the attribute,
and the diff is unchanged.

103
00:07:41,095 --> 00:07:45,970
An attribute that names an undefined driver
has no effect and causes no message.

104
00:07:46,010 --> 00:07:49,841
That is row one of the table,
seen live. Now define the driver.

105
00:07:49,881 --> 00:07:54,756
🟡 CAUTION: git config set diff.<name>.textconv
decides which program Git runs on

106
00:07:54,796 --> 00:07:55,555
your machine.

107
00:07:58,895 --> 00:08:03,847
The program prints only the source of each
cell. With it configured, the header of

108
00:08:03,887 --> 00:08:07,073
the diff still names eda.ipynb
and the real blob IDs.

109
00:08:07,113 --> 00:08:11,456
The body is the difference between
the two renderings: one line of code.

110
00:08:11,796 --> 00:08:15,958
--no-textconv switches the
conversion off. git log -p uses it.

111
00:08:15,998 --> 00:08:21,582
And the patch that format-patch writes, at
the end of the snippet, contains the raw

112
00:08:21,622 --> 00:08:23,751
JSON. A textconv is for reading.

113
00:08:24,091 --> 00:08:27,286
Hunk headers need no
configuration, only the attribute.

114
00:08:27,626 --> 00:08:36,023
The first header says class Accuracy:. After
the attribute diff=python is added, the

115
00:08:36,063 --> 00:08:44,158
second says def update(self, pred, gold):.
The manual lists built-in pattern sets

116
00:08:44,198 --> 00:08:51,088
for python, java, golang, rust,
markdown, bash and about twenty more.

117
00:08:51,128 --> 00:08:57,114
This is also what git log -L
with a function name relies on.

118
00:09:00,454 --> 00:09:03,747
Two conflicts of a kind that
returns at every merge.

119
00:09:03,787 --> 00:09:09,131
Add two attributes: one names a built-in
driver, the other a driver of your own that

120
00:09:09,171 --> 00:09:12,849
doesn't exist yet. Quick quiz:
which file still conflicts?

121
00:09:12,889 --> 00:09:16,374
The changelog, the lock file,
or both. Say it out loud.

122
00:09:21,214 --> 00:09:25,614
The lock file. union merged the
changelog: both new lines are there.

123
00:09:25,654 --> 00:09:30,903
The lock file still conflicts, because
merge=keep-ours names an undefined driver,

124
00:09:30,943 --> 00:09:34,233
and Git fell back to the
text merge without a word.

125
00:09:34,273 --> 00:09:36,322
Row two of the table. Define it.

126
00:09:36,662 --> 00:09:43,236
The driver is the command true: it changes
nothing and exits with 0, so the version

127
00:09:43,276 --> 00:09:47,539
of the current branch stays
in %A. The merge is clean.

128
00:09:47,579 --> 00:09:54,233
And the lock file is the version of main:
the dependency that feat/cache added isn't

129
00:09:54,273 --> 00:10:00,528
in it. A lock file is generated, so the
honest sequence is: keep one side, then

130
00:10:00,568 --> 00:10:04,273
regenerate from the merged
manifest and commit.

131
00:10:04,613 --> 00:10:08,790
Where silently keeping one side
would be wrong, unset the attribute.

132
00:10:09,130 --> 00:10:14,655
"Cannot merge binary files". The path
conflicts, the working file is the version of

133
00:10:14,695 --> 00:10:20,288
the current branch without markers, and the
three stages are in the index for you to

134
00:10:20,328 --> 00:10:21,092
choose from.

135
00:10:21,432 --> 00:10:23,805
One output in the file,
one in the committed blob.

136
00:10:24,145 --> 00:10:28,837
The clean filter reads a notebook on
standard input and writes it without outputs

137
00:10:28,877 --> 00:10:33,510
and execution counts. The smudge filter
is cat: nothing is added on the way out.

138
00:10:33,550 --> 00:10:37,600
The attribute line names the filter,
and the configuration defines it.

139
00:10:39,940 --> 00:10:43,786
The filter is defined. You
have not touched the notebook.

140
00:10:43,826 --> 00:10:45,286
Predict git status -s.

141
00:10:45,626 --> 00:10:49,041
Modified: its cleaned form
differs from the stored blob.

142
00:10:49,081 --> 00:10:52,682
🟡 CAUTION: git add --renormalize
. stages the cleaned form.

143
00:10:52,722 --> 00:10:57,556
After the commit, the blob has no outputs
and your file on disk still has them.

144
00:10:57,896 --> 00:11:02,762
Now run the notebook again. The script
simulates it by changing an execution count.

145
00:11:03,102 --> 00:11:07,785
The file on disk differs from the blob,
and git status and git diff are silent.

146
00:11:07,825 --> 00:11:11,013
That is the purpose. It is
also a trap when you debug.

147
00:11:11,353 --> 00:11:13,582
And now the hook. A teammate clones.

148
00:11:13,922 --> 00:11:18,728
Her clone knows that eda.ipynb has
filter=nbstrip. git config get exits with status

149
00:11:18,768 --> 00:11:22,349
1: there is no filter.nbstrip.clean.
So the filter is a no-op.

150
00:11:22,389 --> 00:11:27,196
She runs the notebook and commits, and the
rest of the snippet shows the outputs in

151
00:11:27,236 --> 00:11:30,407
history. Nothing stripped
them, and nothing warned her.

152
00:11:30,447 --> 00:11:34,437
Row three of the table. Almost any
of us would have made that commit.

153
00:11:36,277 --> 00:11:37,925
Five mistakes to watch for.

154
00:11:38,265 --> 00:11:41,239
"The attribute is committed, so
the filter runs for everyone."

155
00:11:41,279 --> 00:11:45,273
Root cause: the attribute names a driver;
the definition is configuration, which is

156
00:11:45,313 --> 00:11:48,287
not cloned, and a missing definition
is a silent pass-through.

157
00:11:48,627 --> 00:11:52,381
Setting required = true to force
teammates to have the filter.

158
00:11:52,421 --> 00:11:57,398
Root cause: required is part of the definition
that their clone lacks; it protects

159
00:11:57,438 --> 00:11:59,600
against a defined filter that fails.

160
00:11:59,940 --> 00:12:03,450
merge=union on a file where
order or duplicates matter.

161
00:12:03,490 --> 00:12:08,420
Root cause: union keeps the lines of
both sides, in an order the manual calls

162
00:12:08,460 --> 00:12:10,485
random, and reports no conflict.

163
00:12:10,825 --> 00:12:14,166
A keep-ours driver for lock
files with no check afterwards.

164
00:12:14,206 --> 00:12:18,578
Root cause: the other side's dependency
is silently dropped; the file must be

165
00:12:18,618 --> 00:12:21,729
regenerated and CI must verify
it against the manifest.

166
00:12:22,069 --> 00:12:26,883
Trusting a textconv diff as the complete
change. Root cause: a rendering hides what

167
00:12:26,923 --> 00:12:31,502
it leaves out, and it never affects what
is stored or what format-patch writes.

168
00:12:33,342 --> 00:12:38,854
Now, out of the lab. After the 40-megabyte
commit, the team reads the last paragraph

169
00:12:38,894 --> 00:12:42,754
of section 14C.8, which lists
three arrangements that work.

170
00:12:42,794 --> 00:12:48,174
One: a setup step that every clone runs
once executes the git config set filter...

171
00:12:48,214 --> 00:12:53,263
commands. Two: the definition lives in
a tracked configuration file that each

172
00:12:53,303 --> 00:12:54,783
engineer includes once.

173
00:12:55,123 --> 00:12:58,900
Later changes to that file take effect
on pull, which is code execution by pull

174
00:12:58,940 --> 00:13:02,909
request, so it is reviewed like CI
configuration. Three: a tool installs its filter

175
00:13:02,949 --> 00:13:05,566
into the global configuration,
as git lfs install does.

176
00:13:05,906 --> 00:13:10,714
They choose the first and add it to onboarding.
Then they add the part that, in the

177
00:13:10,754 --> 00:13:15,620
textbook's words, can't be skipped in any
of the three: a CI job, an automated check

178
00:13:15,660 --> 00:13:18,891
on the server, that fails when
the stored form is wrong.

179
00:13:18,931 --> 00:13:21,928
The job checks every committed
notebook for outputs.

180
00:13:21,968 --> 00:13:26,776
The filter is now a convenience on each
laptop, and the guarantee is on the server.

181
00:13:28,616 --> 00:13:33,404
Your turn. Do Lab 14.4, "A
clean and smudge filter", in

182
00:13:33,444 --> 00:13:37,090
lab-manual/m14-hooks-rerere-attributes.md.

183
00:13:37,430 --> 00:13:43,100
The lab builds a required pointer filter,
a small model of a large-file extension.

184
00:13:43,140 --> 00:13:48,810
Before each git cat-file -p and each cat
of the working file, predict which of the

185
00:13:48,850 --> 00:13:54,520
two forms you'll see. Before the failure
scenario, in which a teammate without the

186
00:13:54,560 --> 00:13:59,742
definition commits, predict what ends
up in the blob and whether Git prints

187
00:13:59,782 --> 00:14:00,369
anything.

188
00:14:00,709 --> 00:14:06,995
The challenge is Exercise 14.5, Level
2, "A changelog that conflicts on every

189
00:14:07,035 --> 00:14:11,514
merge", in
exercises/m11-m15-investigation-recovery.md.

190
00:14:13,354 --> 00:14:15,285
Answer out loud. I'll wait.

191
00:14:20,125 --> 00:14:24,858
A strong answer first says where each half
lives and which Git operations transfer

192
00:14:24,898 --> 00:14:29,690
it. It then goes through the three cases
and states, for each, exactly what a clone

193
00:14:29,730 --> 00:14:33,009
without the definition does
and whether it says anything.

194
00:14:33,049 --> 00:14:37,491
It treats the required filter separately
and explains why the word "required"

195
00:14:37,531 --> 00:14:39,063
doesn't help in that clone.

196
00:14:39,403 --> 00:14:45,404
It gives the reason Git is designed this
way, in terms of what a cloned repository

197
00:14:45,444 --> 00:14:51,519
must not be able to do to your machine. And
it ends with what a team does about it,

198
00:14:51,559 --> 00:14:55,940
including the one control that
doesn't depend on any laptop.

199
00:14:57,780 --> 00:15:00,707
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

200
00:15:01,047 --> 00:15:05,955
A textconv diff driver changes what I read
in a diff, never what is stored or what a

201
00:15:05,995 --> 00:15:06,839
patch contains.

202
00:15:07,179 --> 00:15:12,216
merge=union keeps both sides' lines and
can be wrong without a conflict; -merge

203
00:15:12,256 --> 00:15:17,100
always conflicts; a custom driver is a
command that leaves its result in %A.

204
00:15:17,440 --> 00:15:22,028
A clean filter rewrites content on the way
into the index and a smudge filter on the

205
00:15:22,068 --> 00:15:24,287
way out; the blob holds the cleaned form.

206
00:15:24,627 --> 00:15:26,447
A clone receives the attribute and not the
definition, and a missing definition is

207
00:15:26,487 --> 00:15:27,241
silent, even for a required filter.

208
00:15:27,581 --> 00:15:32,075
Git LFS and notebook stripping are built
on filters, and the guarantee for both has

209
00:15:32,115 --> 00:15:32,949
to come from CI.

210
00:15:34,789 --> 00:15:39,691
Read sections 14C.6 to
14C.8 of Chapter 14C.

